{"mathdb_id": 331422, "uuid": "9599d637-3a15-43d4-9e8e-bfcbaa5b6af0", "canonical_url": "https://mathdb.com/p/331422/the-sharp-trilinear-inequality-for-antipodally-symmetric-fun", "title": "The sharp trilinear inequality for antipodally symmetric functions on the circle", "statement": "Let $\mathbb{S}^1$ denote the unit circle, let $\mathrm{d}\Sigma_{\vec{\omega}}$ be the measure on $(\mathbb{S}^1)^6$ used in the preceding restriction identity, and let $f\in L^2(\mathbb{S}^1)$ be nonnegative and **antipodally symmetric**, meaning $f(-\omega)=f(\omega)$ for every $\omega\in\mathbb{S}^1$. Then

**The trilinear inequality.**

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\int_{(\mathbb{S}^1)^6} f(\omega_1)f(\omega_2)f(\omega_3)f(\omega_4)f(\omega_5)f(\omega_6)\bigl(|\omega_4+\omega_5+\omega_6|^2-1\bigr)\,\mathrm{d}\Sigma_{\vec{\omega}}\\
&\qquad\leq \int_{(\mathbb{S}^1)^6} f(\omega_1)^2f(\omega_2)^2f(\omega_3)^2\bigl(|\omega_4+\omega_5+\omega_6|^2-1\bigr)\,\mathrm{d}\Sigma_{\vec{\omega}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

